window.docContents[219].resolve({"/Onboarding___-Human-Context-Protocols/L4-___-Out-of-the-line/#A-Property-Kind-Calculus-for-Metrology___-proving-quantity-and-unit-laws-in-Lean___-from-ISO___IEC-80000-to-measurement-uncertainty--Onboarding___-Human-Context-Protocols--L4-___-Out-of-the-line--Small-rigorous-improvement":{"contents":"* Question* Context change* Attribute change* Risk shown* PhysLib stage\n* The wobble throws body A's centre of mass off the line, and a second observer on a moving cart sees the pair differently. What must the model now say about where things are, and for whom?* The pair's centres become points in Space 3; lab F and cart G are named frames.* Position vector, displacement, pair distance, and velocity per frame.* Pair P's distance can be confused with pair Q's, and bare frame vectors accept transport laws that are wrong for the vector's role.* Space Stages 0–4.\n\nThe remaining one-dimensional, one-observer assumption is gone. The no-dissipation\nassumption remains for L5.\n\n","context":"A Property Kind Calculus for Metrology: proving quantity and unit laws in Lean, from ISO/IEC 80000 to measurement uncertainty\u0009Onboarding: Human Context Protocols\u0009L4 — Out of the line","header":"5.5.5. Small rigorous improvement","id":"/Onboarding___-Human-Context-Protocols/L4-___-Out-of-the-line/#A-Property-Kind-Calculus-for-Metrology___-proving-quantity-and-unit-laws-in-Lean___-from-ISO___IEC-80000-to-measurement-uncertainty--Onboarding___-Human-Context-Protocols--L4-___-Out-of-the-line--Small-rigorous-improvement"}});